{"authored" [{"question" "q-122340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of decreasing $x$ where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $4(1+2x)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $1\\,m$ when its velocity is $-4\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "da9200e531b3ab7444cb5a4c08c9cf15199728fb4376cbd0f00077184e7aa70d" "basis" 1577 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/466c0aa389beb6d1a42092b73bae75508ef2c50f1f08cca5efaac20d25615f30.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $4(1+2x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4(1+2x).\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4(1+2x).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int 4(1+2x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=4(x+{x}^{2})+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=1\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=-4\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4(x+{x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(-4)^{2} & =4(1+{1}^{2})+C \\\\ 8 & =8+C \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4(x+{x}^{2})+0 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =8(x+{x}^{2}) \\\\ v & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{2(x+{x}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)<0,$ so we can discard the positive square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=-2\\sqrt{2(x+{x}^{2})}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "da9200e531b3ab7444cb5a4c08c9cf15199728fb4376cbd0f00077184e7aa70d" "basis" 1577 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "785f7610edd208db81cfb7c4ec13a140b5ce47e04ba0e053de3c67f7552cdd94" "basis" 1577 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122340" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "da9200e531b3ab7444cb5a4c08c9cf15199728fb4376cbd0f00077184e7aa70d" "basis" 1577 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122340" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "v(x)=-(2+x+{x}^{2})"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "da9200e531b3ab7444cb5a4c08c9cf15199728fb4376cbd0f00077184e7aa70d" "basis" 1577 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122340" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "v(x)=-2(x+{x}^{2})"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "da9200e531b3ab7444cb5a4c08c9cf15199728fb4376cbd0f00077184e7aa70d" "basis" 1577 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122340" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "v(x)=-2\\sqrt{2(x+{x}^{2})}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "da9200e531b3ab7444cb5a4c08c9cf15199728fb4376cbd0f00077184e7aa70d" "basis" 1577 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122340" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "v(x)=-2\\sqrt{3x+{x}^{2}}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "da9200e531b3ab7444cb5a4c08c9cf15199728fb4376cbd0f00077184e7aa70d" "basis" 1577 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122340" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "v(x)=-\\sqrt{2(3x+5{x}^{2})}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "da9200e531b3ab7444cb5a4c08c9cf15199728fb4376cbd0f00077184e7aa70d" "basis" 1577 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122338" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $(2x+1)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $1\\,m$ when its velocity is $4\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "da9200e531b3ab7444cb5a4c08c9cf15199728fb4376cbd0f00077184e7aa70d" "basis" 1577 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122338" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/983beaf02582c5ebaee129e28bba657eb2db5e4da7f74eb4b8eba73b9083a259.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(2x+1)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2x+1.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2x+1.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={x}^{2}+x+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=1\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=4\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+x+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & ={1}^{2}+1+C \\\\ 8 & =2+C \\\\ C & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+x+6 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({x}^{2}+x+6) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "da9200e531b3ab7444cb5a4c08c9cf15199728fb4376cbd0f00077184e7aa70d" "basis" 1577 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122338" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "785f7610edd208db81cfb7c4ec13a140b5ce47e04ba0e053de3c67f7552cdd94" "basis" 1577 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-122338" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "da9200e531b3ab7444cb5a4c08c9cf15199728fb4376cbd0f00077184e7aa70d" "basis" 1577 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/edb-import/verifi