\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+x| & =t+K \\\\ 1+x & =C{e}^{t} \\\\ x & =C{e}^{t}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{t}-1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6de952276993b9306133268ffd0253be225e386fc93dbc966e7e23f463b0ce1"} {"question" "q-122170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1fbe15c1eaeb00bc90e3707c8cdc9f4f80d60bb81a7c770a978b36974d0cd878"} {"question" "q-122170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6de952276993b9306133268ffd0253be225e386fc93dbc966e7e23f463b0ce1"} {"question" "q-122170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)=1-{e}^{t}"] ["math" "x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+t)"] ["math" "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}t"] ["math" "x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+t)"] ["math" "x(t)={e}^{t}-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6de952276993b9306133268ffd0253be225e386fc93dbc966e7e23f463b0ce1"} {"question" "q-122170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)={e}^{t}-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6de952276993b9306133268ffd0253be225e386fc93dbc966e7e23f463b0ce1"} {"question" "q-122316" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, the velocity is given by $v(x)=\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}\\,m/s.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6de952276993b9306133268ffd0253be225e386fc93dbc966e7e23f463b0ce1"} {"question" "q-122316" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/assets/2093298b6cacb0b29f874d401ccca40560517ff5887763e9a2b88bf4bc051abb.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}} \\\\ (1+x)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int (1+x)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int (1+x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(1+x)^{3} & =t+K \\\\ (1+x)^{3} & =3t+C \\\\ 1+x & =\\sqrt[3]{3t+C} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{3t+C}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\sqrt[3]{3t+1}-1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6de952276993b9306133268ffd0253be225e386fc93dbc966e7e23f463b0ce1"} {"question" "q-122316" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1fbe15c1eaeb00bc90e3707c8cdc9f4f80d60bb81a7c770a978b36974d0cd878"} {"question" "q-122316" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6de952276993b9306133268ffd0253be225e386fc93dbc966e7e23f463b0ce1"} {"question" "q-122316" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)=2\\sqrt[3]{3t+1}-2"] ["math" "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{(t+1)^{2}}-1"] ["math" "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{1-t}-1"] ["math" "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{3t+1}-1"] ["math" "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{3t-1}+1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6de952276993b9306133268ffd0253be225e386fc93dbc966e7e23f463b0ce1"} {"question" "q-122316" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{3t+1}-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6de952276993b9306133268ffd0253be225e386fc93dbc966e7e23f463b0ce1"} {"question" "q-122338" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $(2x+1)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $1\\,m$ when its velocity is $4\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6de952276993b9306133268ffd0253be225e386fc93dbc966e7e23f463b0ce1"} {"question" "q-122338" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/assets/983beaf02582c5ebaee129e28bba657eb2db5e4da7f74eb4b8eba73b9083a259.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(2x+1)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2x+1.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2x+1.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={x}^{2}+x+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=1\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=4\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+x+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & ={1}^{2}+1+C \\\\ 8 & =2+C \\\\ C & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+x+6 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({x}^{2}+x+6) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6de952276993b9306133268ffd0253be225e386fc93dbc966e7e23f463b