"/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6de952276993b9306133268ffd0253be225e386fc93dbc966e7e23f463b0ce1"} {"question" "q-122340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/assets/466c0aa389beb6d1a42092b73bae75508ef2c50f1f08cca5efaac20d25615f30.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $4(1+2x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4(1+2x).\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4(1+2x).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int 4(1+2x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=4(x+{x}^{2})+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=1\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=-4\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4(x+{x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(-4)^{2} & =4(1+{1}^{2})+C \\\\ 8 & =8+C \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4(x+{x}^{2})+0 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =8(x+{x}^{2}) \\\\ v & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{2(x+{x}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)<0,$ so we can discard the positive square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=-2\\sqrt{2(x+{x}^{2})}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6de952276993b9306133268ffd0253be225e386fc93dbc966e7e23f463b0ce1"} {"question" "q-122340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1fbe15c1eaeb00bc90e3707c8cdc9f4f80d60bb81a7c770a978b36974d0cd878"} {"question" "q-122340" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6de952276993b9306133268ffd0253be225e386fc93dbc966e7e23f463b0ce1"} {"question" "q-122340" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "v(x)=-(2+x+{x}^{2})"] ["math" "v(x)=-2(x+{x}^{2})"] ["math" "v(x)=-2\\sqrt{2(x+{x}^{2})}"] ["math" "v(x)=-2\\sqrt{3x+{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "v(x)=-\\sqrt{2(3x+5{x}^{2})}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6de952276993b9306133268ffd0253be225e386fc93dbc966e7e23f463b0ce1"} {"question" "q-122340" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "v(x)=-2\\sqrt{2(x+{x}^{2})}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6de952276993b9306133268ffd0253be225e386fc93dbc966e7e23f463b0ce1"} {"question" "q-122346" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x,$ away from a fixed origin $O.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $(2+\\frac{x}{2}){m/s}^{2}.$ If $v=5\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=2\\,\\text{m}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6de952276993b9306133268ffd0253be225e386fc93dbc966e7e23f463b0ce1"} {"question" "q-122346" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/assets/d9482b116bad201dec2c71f173bf18d7dc2f170acbb37052151bba38a7898450.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(2+\\frac{x}{2}){m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2+\\frac{x}{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2+\\frac{x}{2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int (2+\\frac{x}{2})\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=2x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=5\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(5)^{2} & =2(0)+\\frac{{0}^{2}}{4}+C \\\\ \\frac{25}{2} & =0+0+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{25}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{25}{2} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+4x+25 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+4x+25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+4x+25}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}+4(2)+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2+8+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{35} \\\\  & ≈5.9\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6de952276993b9306133268ffd0253be225e386fc93dbc966e7e23f463b0ce1"} {"question" "q-122346" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1fbe15c1eaeb00bc90e3707c8cdc9f4f80d60bb81a7c770a978b36974d0cd878"} {"question" "q-122346" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6de952276993b9306133268ffd0253be225e386fc93dbc966e7e23f463b0ce1"} {"question" "q-122346" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "5.1\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "5.5\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "5.9\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "6.3\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "6.8\\,\\text{m/s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6de952276993b9306133268ffd0253be225e386fc93dbc966e7e23f463b0ce1"} {"question" "q-122346" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5.9\\,\\text{m/s}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6de952276993b9306133268ffd0253be225e386fc93dbc966e7e23f463b0ce1"}] "usage" {"q-122340" [] "q-122170" [] "q-122316" [] "q-122338" []} "basis" 2518 "retractions" [[17592186167021 :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/assets/a8a6f49ead700bee856b1bbdbb10848eac0a57609da14e3967916a36eb56f048.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)=1+x.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1+x.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1+x \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+x| & =t+K \\\\ 1+x & =C{e}^{t} \\\\ x & =C{e}^{t}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{t}-1.\n$$"] [17592186317649 :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/2093298b6cacb0b29f874d401ccca40560517ff5887763e9a2b88bf4bc051abb.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}} \\\\ (1+x)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int (1+x)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int (1+x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(1+x)^{3} & =t+K \\\\ (1+x)^{3} & =3t+C \\\\ 1+x & =\\sqrt[3]{3t+C} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{3t+C}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\sqrt[3]{3t+1}-1.\n$$"] [17592186317670 :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/983beaf02582c5ebaee129e28bba657eb2db5e4da7f74eb4b8eba73b9083a259.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(2x+1)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2x+1.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2x+1.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={x}^{2}+x+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=1\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=4\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+x+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & ={1}^{2}+1+C \\\\ 8 & =2+C \\\\ C & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+x+6 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({x}^{2}+x+6) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}.\n$$"] [17592186317684 :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/466c0aa389beb6d1a42092b73bae75508ef2c50f1f08cca5efaac20d25615f30.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $4(1+2x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4(1+2x).\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4(1+2x).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int 4(1+2x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=4(x+{x}^{2})+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=1\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=-4\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4(x+{x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(-4)^{2} & =4(1+{1}^{2})+C \\\\ 8 & =8+C \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4(x+{x}^{2})+0 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =8(x+{x}^{2}) \\\\ v & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{2(x+{x}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)<0,$ so we can discard the positive square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=-2\\sqrt{2(x+{x}^{2})}.\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186163545 17592186163546 17592186163547 17592186163548 17592186163549 17592186317651 17592186317652 17592186317653 17592186317654 17592186317655 17592186317672 17592186317673 17592186317674 17592186317675 17592186317676 17592186317686 17592186317687 17592186317688 17592186317689 17592186317690] "immutable_fields" [[17592186163544 :answer-field/correct] [17592186163544 :answer-field/id] [17592186163544 :answer-field/key] [17592186163544 :answer-field/type] [17592186317650 :answer-field/correct] [17592186317650 :answer-field/id] [17592186317650 :answer-field/key] [17592186317650 :answer-field/type] [17592186317671 :answer-field/correct] [17592186317671 :answer-field/id] [17592186317671 :answer-field/key] [17592186317671 :answer-field/type] [17592186317685 :answer-field/correct] [17592186317685 :answer-field/id] [17592186317685 :answer-field/key] [17592186317685 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
